# Every pattern here is matched against the WHOLE path relative to the build
# context, using Go's filepath.Match plus `**`. That has one consequence worth
# stating loudly, because getting it wrong is invisible until an image is a
# gigabyte too big:
#
#   `node_modules` excludes ONLY the root node_modules.
#   `**/node_modules` excludes every node_modules at every depth.
#
# This file used to say `.next`, which excluded the root one and let
# `apps/web/.next` through — around a gigabyte of Turbopack DEV output, copied
# into the builder by `COPY apps ./apps` and then into the runtime image by
# `COPY --from=builder /app/apps/web/.next`. The production build writes into
# the same directory without clearing it, so the dev artefacts simply rode
# along. Keep the `**/` prefixes.

.git
.gitignore
*.md
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.DS_Store
.vscode
.idea
*.swp
*.swo
.legacy
.claude

# Build output. The builder stage CREATES .next; it must never receive one,
# least of all a dev server's.
**/.next
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Dependencies. Every stage runs its own `pnpm install`, so a copied
# node_modules is at best ignored and at worst the wrong platform's binaries.
**/node_modules
npm-debug.log
*.log

# pnpm's local content-addressable store — hundreds of megabytes that only
# slow the context transfer down; the install inside each stage fetches its
# own.
.pnpm-store

# Test and screenshot output.
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
**/coverage

# Vendored API specifications and design notes. Read by a test and by people,
# never by a build — and the PDFs and OpenAPI dumps are tens of megabytes.
docs
